# Multi-agent workflow (agentkit)
The main session owns planning, approvals and Agent management. Approved parents and children follow the Family assignments section within their assigned scope.

Before launching any agent, including supervisor-plan and supervisor-review, verify the live registration in the active Codex configuration containing this workflow. Read the selected role's `config_file` from that `config.toml` and check for a shadowing definition in the running host. Use that role's `developer_instructions`, `model`, `model_reasoning_effort` and sandbox settings. If effort is omitted, verify the host's effective inherited effort. Ordinary top-level assignments use the installed role's selected model settings, including a configured cross-provider relay.
- Select the registered role explicitly when the host supports registered roles. A task name or prompt leaves role settings to the host defaults.
- For a prompt-based launch, pass the installed instructions and apply the selected role's settings. For `collaboration.spawn_agent`, use `fork_turns="none"` with explicit model or effort overrides and supply all task context in the message. Reuse an existing thread only while its launch settings and scope still match.

For any task that changes code or has several steps (not quick questions or one-line edits), I authorize subagents, with this process:
1. Run the supervisor-plan agent. It splits the task into parts and parallel waves. Skip supervisor-plan and do the task directly if it touches 3 files or fewer, in one area, with no schema or auth change. Say so in one line.
2. Show me the plan: number of agents, waves, and what each agent does. Ask me the option for each part marked NEEDS_SKILL_CHOICE. If I pick shadcn, name package.json and the config files in the assignment. If a part needs an install, ask me to approve the packages, and write `Install approved: <packages>` in the assignment. Wait for my approval before you start any worker. I can change the plan.
3. Start the approved workers as Codex subagents. Use the agent role that matches each part. Run parts in the same wave in parallel. Two parts never edit the same file.
4. Send the original task, all worker reports and the base SHA (HEAD before step 3, if the project uses git) to the supervisor-review agent.
5. For each FIX, send it to the worker that owns the part, through its parent when a family did the work. Prefer the prior thread when its settings and scope still match. Otherwise relaunch the same role with the earlier report and FIX under the same controls. Then run supervisor-review again. Stop after 2 rounds and report what is open.
6. Tell me the result, the verdict, and anything unresolved.
Pick agent roles by task: supervisor-plan, supervisor-review, backend, frontend, mobile, design, marketing, writing, security, seo, devops, docs, database, researcher, tester, debugger, planner (PRDs and plans), architect, payments, workflow-optimizer, product.
